\title{Equivalences}
\taxon{Type Theory}
\date{2026-07-18}
\import{macros}
\p{
    This note follows Chapter 9 of [Introduction to Homotopy Type Theory](hott-2022).
    Equivalence is the correct notion of sameness between types; its definition must eventually be
    shown to be a property of a map rather than extra, non-unique structure.
}
\subtree{
    \title{Homotopies}
    \transclude{def-0059}
    \p{
        Homotopies express commutative diagrams in type theory. They have reflexivity, inverse, and
        concatenation operations inherited pointwise from identity types.
    }
}
\subtree{
    \title{Bi-invertible maps}
    \transclude{def-005G}
    \transclude{def-005H}
    \transclude{def-005B}
    \transclude{def-005C}
    \transclude{def-005I}
    \p{
        It is tempting to define an equivalence using a single inverse. The problem is that
        #{\mathsf{hasInverse}(f)} can contain nontrivial higher information, whereas
        #{\mathsf{isEquiv}(f)} will be shown, using function extensionality, to be a proposition.
        For example, a later calculation gives
        #{\mathsf{hasInverse}(\mathsf{id}_{\mathbb{S}^1})\simeq\mathbb{Z}}.
    }
    \p{
        Consequently, the following two constructions establish logical equivalence, but they do
        not assert that the witness types #{\mathsf{hasInverse}(f)} and
        #{\mathsf{isEquiv}(f)} are themselves equivalent. The composites of the two constructions
        need not be homotopic to the corresponding identity maps.
    }
    \transclude{thm-0015}
    \transclude{thm-0016}
    \p{
        Identity maps, composites, and inverse maps are equivalences. Equivalences satisfy
        two-out-of-three, so in
        a composite, any two of the three maps being equivalences forces the third to be one.
    }
}
\subtree{
    \title{Identities of dependent pairs}
    \p{
        For #{u=(x,a)} and #{v=(y,b)} in #{\Sigma_{x:A}B(x)}, an identity consists of an
        identity in the base and a compatible identity in the fiber:
        ##{
            (u=v)\simeq
            \Sigma_{p:x=y}\bigl(\mathsf{transport}_B(p,a)=b\bigr).
        }
        This equivalence is the basic tool for calculating equality in structured objects. It also
        explains why equality of ordinary pairs is componentwise only after accounting for
        dependency.
    }
}
